{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011VWtBqTcAVaVpJhQR8pHZa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Nano-30B-A3B-FP8\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Fused intra-chunk diagonal computation for Mamba2 SSD that combines segment_sum for causal masking, B/C matrix contraction to form attention-like weights G, and application of exponential decay mask L to compute Y_diag. This is the most memory-intensive part of the chunked parallel scan.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_size\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n8\t            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n9\t        },\n10\t        \"num_chunks\": {\n11\t            \"type\": \"var\",\n12\t            \"description\": \"Number of chunks in sequence\"\n13\t        },\n14\t        \"chunk_size\": {\n15\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n16\t            \"value\": 128,\n17\t            \"description\": \"Size of each chunk\"\n18\t        },\n19\t        \"num_heads\": {\n20\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n21\t            \"value\": 32,\n22\t            \"description\": \"Number of attention heads\"\n23\t        },\n24\t        \"head_dim\": {\n25\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n26\t            \"value\": 128,\n27\t            \"description\": \"Dimension per head\"\n28\t        },\n29\t        \"n_groups\": {\n30\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n31\t            \"value\": 8,\n32\t            \"description\": \"Number of groups for B/C matrices\"\n33\t        },\n34\t        \"state_size\": {\n35\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n36\t            \"value\": 128,\n37\t            \"description\": \"SSM state dimension\"\n38\t        }\n39\t    },\n40\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n41\t    \"inputs\": {\n42\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n43\t            \"shape\": [\n44\t                \"batch_size\",\n45\t                \"num_chunks\",\n46\t                \"chunk_size\",\n47\t                \"num_heads\",\n48\t                \"head_dim\"\n49\t            ],\n50\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n51\t            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n52\t        },\n53\t        \"A_cumsum\": {\n54\t            \"shape\": [\n55\t                \"batch_size\",\n56\t                \"num_heads\",\n57\t                \"num_chunks\",\n58\t                \"chunk_size\"\n59\t            ],\n60\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n61\t            \"description\": \"Cumulative sum of discretized A matrix\"\n62\t        },\n63\t        \"B\": {\n64\t            \"shape\": [\n65\t                \"batch_size\",\n66\t                \"num_chunks\",\n67\t                \"chunk_size\",\n68\t                \"n_groups\",\n69\t                \"state_size\"\n70\t            ],\n71\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n72\t            \"description\": \"B matrix for state update\"\n73\t        },\n74\t        \"C\": {\n75\t            \"shape\": [\n76\t                \"batch_size\",\n77\t                \"num_chunks\",\n78\t                \"chunk_size\",\n79\t                \"n_groups\",\n80\t                \"state_size\"\n81\t            ],\n82\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n83\t            \"description\": \"C matrix for output projection\"\n84\t        }\n85\t    },\n86\t    \"outputs\": {\n87\t        \"Y_diag\": {\n88\t            \"shape\": [\n89\t                \"batch_size\",\n90\t                \"num_chunks\",\n91\t                \"chunk_size\",\n92\t                \"num_heads\",\n93\t                \"head_dim\"\n94\t            ],\n95\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n96\t            \"description\": \"Intra-chunk diagonal output\"\n97\t        }\n98\t    },\n99\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    A_cumsum: torch.Tensor,\\n    B: torch.Tensor,\\n    C: torch.Tensor\\n) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Compute intra-chunk diagonal output Y_diag for Mamba2 SSD.\\n    \\n    Steps:\\n    1. Compute segment_sum(A) to get cumulative decay factors\\n    2. Apply exp() to get causal mask L with exponential decay\\n    3. Contract B and C matrices to form attention weights G\\n    4. Apply mask: M = G * L (element-wise)\\n    5. Compute output: Y_diag = sum(M * hidden_states) over sequence dimension\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    # Constants\\n    CHUNK_SIZE = 128\\n    NUM_HEADS = 32\\n    N_GROUPS = 8\\n    \\n    batch_size, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim = hidden_states.shape\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Compute segment_sum with lower triangular masking for causality\\n    # A_cumsum: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Expand to add target dimension\\n    # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size] -> [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    A_expanded = A_cumsum[..., None].expand(*A_cumsum.size(), CHUNK_SIZE).to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Create lower triangular mask (diagonal = -1 means exclude diagonal)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(CHUNK_SIZE, CHUNK_SIZE, device=A_cumsum.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=-1\\n    )\\n    \\n    # Zero out upper triangular part\\n    A_masked = A_expanded.masked_fill(~mask, 0)\\n    \\n    # Compute cumulative sum along the source dimension\\n    A_cumsum_seg = torch.cumsum(A_masked, dim=-2)\\n    \\n    # Create mask including diagonal this time\\n    mask_with_diag = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(CHUNK_SIZE, CHUNK_SIZE, device=A_cumsum.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=0\\n    )\\n    \\n    # Mask out upper triangular part of cumsum result\\n    segment_sum_A = A_cumsum_seg.masked_fill(~mask_with_diag, -torch.inf)\\n    \\n    # Apply exponential to get causal decay mask L\\n    # L[i,j] represents decay from position j to position i (i >= j)\\n    L = torch.exp(segment_sum_A)\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Contract B and C to form attention-like weights G\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size]\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size]\\n    \\n    # Convert to float32 for computation\\n    B_f32 = B.to(torch.float32)\\n    C_f32 = C.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Expand B and C from n_groups to num_heads\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size] -> [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    B_expanded = B_f32.repeat_interleave(NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS, dim=3)\\n    C_expanded = C_f32.repeat_interleave(NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS, dim=3)\\n    \\n    # Compute G via outer product and sum over state dimension\\n    # G[i,j] = sum_n(C[i,n] * B[j,n])\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # Result: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Add dimension for broadcasting\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, 1, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, 1, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    C_for_G = C_expanded[:, :, :, None, :, :]\\n    B_for_G = B_expanded[:, :, None, :, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply and sum over state dimension\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    G = (C_for_G * B_for_G).sum(dim=-1)\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Apply causal mask L to G to get M\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Permute L to match G dimensions\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size] -> [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    L_permuted = L.permute(0, 2, 3, 4, 1)\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply\\n    M = G * L_permuted\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Step 4: Compute Y_diag by applying M to hidden_states\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    # hidden_states: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    # Result: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    hidden_states_f32 = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Add dimension to M for head_dim\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads, 1]\\n    M_expanded = M[..., None]\\n    \\n    # Add dimension to hidden_states for chunk_size_i\\n    # hidden_states: [batch, num_chunks, 1, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    hidden_states_expanded = hidden_states_f32[:, :, None, :, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply and sum over chunk_size_j dimension\\n    Y_diag = (M_expanded * hidden_states_expanded).sum(dim=3)\\n    # Y_diag: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    return Y_diag.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n\"\n100\t}\n101\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"186381ab-5278-4fc8-9646-c8c71e3836fb","uuid":"55711939-592b-4994-a5ea-5928f92bbb83","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:47:29.323Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3-Nano-30B-A3B-FP8\",\n    \"description\": \"Fused intra-chunk diagonal computation for Mamba2 SSD that combines segment_sum for causal masking, B/C matrix contraction to form attention-like weights G, and application of exponential decay mask L to compute Y_diag. This is the most memory-intensive part of the chunked parallel scan.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Batch size\"\n        },\n        \"num_chunks\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\",\n            \"description\": \"Number of chunks in sequence\"\n        },\n        \"chunk_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 128,\n            \"description\": \"Size of each chunk\"\n        },\n        \"num_heads\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 32,\n            \"description\": \"Number of attention heads\"\n        },\n        \"head_dim\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 128,\n            \"description\": \"Dimension per head\"\n        },\n        \"n_groups\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 8,\n            \"description\": \"Number of groups for B/C matrices\"\n        },\n        \"state_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 128,\n            \"description\": \"SSM state dimension\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_chunks\",\n                \"chunk_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"head_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input hidden states\"\n        },\n        \"A_cumsum\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"num_chunks\",\n                \"chunk_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Cumulative sum of discretized A matrix\"\n        },\n        \"B\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_chunks\",\n                \"chunk_size\",\n                \"n_groups\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"B matrix for state update\"\n        },\n        \"C\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_chunks\",\n                \"chunk_size\",\n                \"n_groups\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"C matrix for output projection\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"Y_diag\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_chunks\",\n                \"chunk_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"head_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Intra-chunk diagonal output\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    A_cumsum: torch.Tensor,\\n    B: torch.Tensor,\\n    C: torch.Tensor\\n) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    Compute intra-chunk diagonal output Y_diag for Mamba2 SSD.\\n    \\n    Steps:\\n    1. Compute segment_sum(A) to get cumulative decay factors\\n    2. Apply exp() to get causal mask L with exponential decay\\n    3. Contract B and C matrices to form attention weights G\\n    4. Apply mask: M = G * L (element-wise)\\n    5. Compute output: Y_diag = sum(M * hidden_states) over sequence dimension\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    # Constants\\n    CHUNK_SIZE = 128\\n    NUM_HEADS = 32\\n    N_GROUPS = 8\\n    \\n    batch_size, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim = hidden_states.shape\\n    \\n    # Step 1: Compute segment_sum with lower triangular masking for causality\\n    # A_cumsum: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Expand to add target dimension\\n    # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size] -> [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    A_expanded = A_cumsum[..., None].expand(*A_cumsum.size(), CHUNK_SIZE).to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Create lower triangular mask (diagonal = -1 means exclude diagonal)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(CHUNK_SIZE, CHUNK_SIZE, device=A_cumsum.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=-1\\n    )\\n    \\n    # Zero out upper triangular part\\n    A_masked = A_expanded.masked_fill(~mask, 0)\\n    \\n    # Compute cumulative sum along the source dimension\\n    A_cumsum_seg = torch.cumsum(A_masked, dim=-2)\\n    \\n    # Create mask including diagonal this time\\n    mask_with_diag = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(CHUNK_SIZE, CHUNK_SIZE, device=A_cumsum.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=0\\n    )\\n    \\n    # Mask out upper triangular part of cumsum result\\n    segment_sum_A = A_cumsum_seg.masked_fill(~mask_with_diag, -torch.inf)\\n    \\n    # Apply exponential to get causal decay mask L\\n    # L[i,j] represents decay from position j to position i (i >= j)\\n    L = torch.exp(segment_sum_A)\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Step 2: Contract B and C to form attention-like weights G\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size]\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size]\\n    \\n    # Convert to float32 for computation\\n    B_f32 = B.to(torch.float32)\\n    C_f32 = C.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Expand B and C from n_groups to num_heads\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, n_groups, state_size] -> [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    B_expanded = B_f32.repeat_interleave(NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS, dim=3)\\n    C_expanded = C_f32.repeat_interleave(NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS, dim=3)\\n    \\n    # Compute G via outer product and sum over state dimension\\n    # G[i,j] = sum_n(C[i,n] * B[j,n])\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # Result: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Add dimension for broadcasting\\n    # C: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, 1, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B: [batch, num_chunks, 1, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    C_for_G = C_expanded[:, :, :, None, :, :]\\n    B_for_G = B_expanded[:, :, None, :, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply and sum over state dimension\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    G = (C_for_G * B_for_G).sum(dim=-1)\\n    \\n    # Step 3: Apply causal mask L to G to get M\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Permute L to match G dimensions\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size] -> [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    L_permuted = L.permute(0, 2, 3, 4, 1)\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply\\n    M = G * L_permuted\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Step 4: Compute Y_diag by applying M to hidden_states\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    # hidden_states: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    # Result: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    hidden_states_f32 = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Add dimension to M for head_dim\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads, 1]\\n    M_expanded = M[..., None]\\n    \\n    # Add dimension to hidden_states for chunk_size_i\\n    # hidden_states: [batch, num_chunks, 1, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    hidden_states_expanded = hidden_states_f32[:, :, None, :, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Element-wise multiply and sum over chunk_size_j dimension\\n    Y_diag = (M_expanded * hidden_states_expanded).sum(dim=3)\\n    # Y_diag: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    return Y_diag.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":101,"startLine":1,"totalLines":101}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TByKMXBBH1fQMBmAfwFwEo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/070 Mamba2 Fused Intra-Chunk Diagonal Computation\n2\t\n3\tTask: optimize the official SOL-ExecBench task\n4\t`L1/070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation` on **NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**.\n5\tSubmission is `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(hidden_states, A_cumsum, B, C) -> Y_diag`.\n6\tPrimary implementation must be **Triton** (PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing; no\n7\tTorch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback).\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. What the operation actually computes\n12\t\n13\t### 1.1 Shapes and constants (from `task/definition.json`)\n14\t\n15\tConstants (fixed across all workloads):\n16\t\n17\t| name        | value | role                      |\n18\t|-------------|-------|---------------------------|\n19\t| CHUNK_SIZE  | 128   | intra-chunk sequence len  |\n20\t| NUM_HEADS   | 32    | attention heads           |\n21\t| HEAD_DIM    | 128   | per-head feature dim (V/out) |\n22\t| N_GROUPS    | 8     | groups for B/C            |\n23\t| STATE_SIZE  | 128   | SSM state dim (contraction dim of G) |\n24\t\n25\tDerived: `heads_per_group = NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS = 4`, i.e. head `h` belongs to group\n26\t`g = h // 4`.\n27\t\n28\tVariable axes: `batch_size (B)` and `num_chunks (C)`.\n29\t\n30\tInputs (all `bfloat16`):\n31\t- `hidden_states` (X): `[B, C, 128(s), 32(h), 128(d)]`\n32\t- `A_cumsum`      (a): `[B, 32(h), C, 128(s)]`\n33\t- `B` (Bmat):          `[B, C, 128(s), 8(g), 128(n)]`\n34\t- `C` (Cmat):          `[B, C, 128(s), 8(g), 128(n)]`\n35\t\n36\tOutput (`bfloat16`):\n37\t- `Y_diag` (Y): `[B, C, 128(s), 32(h), 128(d)]`  (same layout as `hidden_states`)\n38\t\n39\t### 1.2 Per-(batch b, chunk c, head h) math\n40\t\n41\tThe reference expands B/C over heads via `repeat_interleave(4, dim=groups)`, so group\n42\t`g = h // 4`. For each independent `(b, c, h)` triple define, over local indices\n43\t`i, j ∈ [0,128)` (query row `i`, key/value row `j`) and state/feature index `n, d`:\n44\t\n45\t- `Cmat = C[b, c, :, g, :]`  → `[128(i), 128(n)]`\n46\t- `Bmat = B[b, c, :, g, :]`  → `[128(j), 128(n)]`\n47\t- `a    = A_cumsum[b, h, c, :]` → `[128]`\n48\t- `X    = hidden_states[b, c, :, h, :]` → `[128(j), 128(d)]`\n49\t\n50\t**Step 1 — decay mask `L` (segment_sum):**\n51\tThe reference *re-cumsums* the input `A_cumsum` inside the chunk. Working it through the\n52\t`tril(diagonal=-1)` masked cumsum + `tril(diagonal=0)` `-inf` fill:\n53\t\n54\t```\n55\tsegsum[i, j] = sum_{k=j+1..i} a[k]           for i >= j     (diagonal i==j → empty sum → 0)\n56\tsegsum[i, j] = -inf                          for i <  j\n57\tL[i, j]      = exp(segsum[i, j])             (i<j → 0, i==j → 1)\n58\t```\n59\t\n60\tEquivalently, with prefix sum `cA[i] = sum_{k=0..i} a[k]`:\n61\t```\n62\tL[i, j] = exp(cA[i] - cA[j]) for i >= j, else 0.\n63\t```\n64\t`L` is **lower-triangular (incl. diagonal)** in `(i, j)`, and is **per-head** (because `a`\n65\tdepends on `h`). NOTE: the input is *named* `A_cumsum` but the reference applies a further\n66\tcumulative sum to it — we must reproduce that extra cumsum, not treat the input as already\n67\tbeing `cA`.\n68\t\n69\t**Step 2 — scores `G` (state contraction):**\n70\t```\n71\tG[i, j] = sum_n Cmat[i, n] * Bmat[j, n]   →   G = Cmat @ Bmat^T   ([128,128])\n72\t```\n73\t`G` depends only on `(b, c, g)` — it is **shared by the 4 heads of a group**.\n74\t\n75\t**Step 3 — masked weights `M`:**\n76\t```\n77\tM[i, j] = G[i, j] * L[i, j]     (lower-triangular, per-head)\n78\t```\n79\t\n80\t**Step 4 — output:**\n81\t```\n82\tY[i, d] = sum_j M[i, j] * X[j, d]   →   Y = M @ X   ([128,128])\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\t### 1.3 One-line characterization\n86\t\n87\tThis is **masked \"attention\" per `(b, c, h)`** with:\n88\t`Q≡Cmat`, `K≡Bmat` (contraction over `state_size`), scores `G = Q·Kᵀ`, an\n89\texponential **causal decay mask** `L` (no softmax / no normalization), `V≡X`, output\n90\t`Y = (G ∘ L) · X`. Every reduction dimension (state, kv-seq, head_dim) is exactly **128**.\n91\tThere are `B·C·32` independent `[128×128]` output tiles; each costs two\n92\t`128×128×128` matmuls (`G`, then `Y`).\n93\t\n94\t### 1.4 Feedback workload sizes\n95\t\n96\t`(B, C)` ∈ {(2,4), (1,3), (4,4), (4,7), (1,4)} → `B·C·H` programs (per-head layout) ∈\n97\t{256, 96, 512, 896, 128}. Largest is `B·C=28` → 896 head-tiles. With 108 SMs on A800 that\n98\tis ~2–8 waves; parallelism is adequate even before any grouping.\n99\t\n100\t---\n101\t\n102\t## 2. Constraints\n103\t\n104\t- **Triton-only compute**; PyTorch allowed for shapes/strides/empty-alloc/launch only.\n105\t- **No fallback** of any kind (Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext/alternate). A failing Triton kernel\n106\t  is simply invalid.\n107\t- Output must be `bfloat16`, exact shape `[B, C, 128, 32, 128]`, same layout as input `X`.\n108\t- **Cannot run** CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/external evaluator/alternate correctness harness\n109\t  directly. The *only* correctness+perf signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n110\t  → Validation must lean heavily on **static analysis and faithful math reproduction**, with\n111\t  each candidate confirmed through the official feedback evaluator.\n112\t- Immutable, sequential candidates `c001, c002, …`; one kernel version over all 5 feedback\n113\t  workloads = one evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft/hard 1.0M / 1.2M.\n114\t- Metric: **geometric-mean speedup**, and **every** selected workload must pass correctness.\n115\t- Skills: `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100 profiling) are\n116\t  **out of scope** for an Ampere `sm_80` target and are additionally blocked by the\n117\t  \"no profiler\" rule; I will not invoke them. All other external agents/tools are forbidden.\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 3. Numerical risks and how to match the reference\n122\t\n123\tTolerance (all 5 feedback workloads): `max_atol = 1e-5`, `max_rtol = 0.05` (assumed combined,\n124\t`|out-ref| <= atol + rtol·|ref|`). `rtol = 5%` is loose; `atol = 1e-5` only bites near zero.\n125\t\n126\tReference numeric path: everything is promoted to **fp32** for the arithmetic (A, B, C, X all\n127\tcast to fp32), `L = exp(...)` in fp32, and the final `Y` is cast to bf16 on return.\n128\t\n129\t1. **Exponential dynamic range (main risk).** Inputs are `random` (≈ N(0,1) bf16). `a` is not\n130\t   guaranteed negative, so `cA` behaves like a random walk (std ~`sqrt(128)≈11`), and\n131\t   `cA[i]-cA[j]` for `i>=j` can reach ±(30–60). `exp(60)≈1e26` is finite in fp32 (max ≈3.4e38)\n132\t   but **huge**; extreme tails could overflow to `inf`. Mitigations / rules:\n133\t   - Compute `exp` of the **difference** `segsum[i,j] = cA[i]-cA[j]` directly (as the reference\n134\t     does), **never** as `exp(cA[i])·exp(-cA[j])` — the factored form overflows/underflows even\n135\t     when the difference is bounded (`cA[i]` alone can be ±40).\n136\t   - **Do NOT** use the FlashAttention max-subtraction / online-rescale trick. There is **no\n137\t     softmax normalization here**, so subtracting a row max changes the result (it would not\n138\t     cancel). We must accumulate the true weighted sum. (We may still *tile* the kv loop, just\n139\t     without renormalization.)\n140\t   - Because both our kernel and the reference form the same `exp(difference)` in fp32, `inf`\n141\t     outputs (if any) should coincide; but `inf`/`nan` are a correctness landmine — watch the\n142\t     first feedback run for NaN/Inf failures and, if needed, keep the exp argument computation\n143\t     bit-faithful to the reference ordering.\n144\t\n145\t2. **Matmul-1 (`G = Cmat @ Bmatᵀ`).** Reference: bf16→fp32 (lossless) then fp32 MAC. Triton\n146\t   `tl.dot(bf16, bf16, out=fp32)` uses bf16 tensor cores with fp32 accumulate; bf16×bf16 exact\n147\t   products + fp32 accumulate ≈ the reference to well within `rtol`. Acceptable.\n148\t\n149\t3. **Matmul-2 (`Y = M @ X`).** `M = G∘L` is fp32 with large dynamic range; `X` is bf16 (the\n150\t   reference casts it to fp32 losslessly). Two options:\n151\t   - **bf16 tensor-core** (`M→bf16`, `X→bf16`, fp32 accum): fast; per-element relative error of\n152\t     `M` ≈ 2⁻⁸ ≈ 0.4% — comfortably inside `rtol=0.05`. Since each output element is dominated\n153\t     by the largest-`L` terms (same as the reference fp32 sum), small terms vanish in both.\n154\t   - **fp32/TF32** (`M` fp32, `X`→fp32, `tl.dot` TF32 ~10-bit): closer to the reference but\n155\t     slower. Keep as a correctness fallback if bf16 proves marginal on any workload.\n156\t   Default plan: bf16 tensor core; escalate to TF32 only if a workload fails.\n157\t\n158\t4. **Near-zero atol.** Rows with few unmasked terms (e.g. row `i=0` has only `j=0`,\n159\t   `Y[0,d]=G[0,0]·X[0,d]`) are single products computed almost identically in both paths →\n160\t   safe. The bf16 output quantization the reference itself applies (~value·2⁻⁸) dominates and\n161\t   is inside tolerance.\n162\t\n163\t5. **Group sharing correctness.** If a candidate computes `G` once per group and reuses it for\n164\t   4 heads, the reuse is exact (`G` truly is head-independent); only `L` and `X` differ per\n165\t   head. Must index `A_cumsum[b,h,c,:]` and `X[...,h,:]` per head, but `Bmat/Cmat` per group.\n166\t\n167\t---\n168\t\n169\t## 4. Triton design space\n170\t\n171\tBecause `chunk = state = head_dim = 128`, every tile is a clean multiple of 128; no ragged\n172\tremainders. The op is **memory-heavy**: `X` and `Y` are the big tensors\n173\t(`B·C·32·128·128·2 B` ≈ 59 MB each at `B·C=28`), while `B`/`C` are 4× smaller (8 groups) and\n174\t`A_cumsum` is tiny. So minimizing redundant `X`/`Y` traffic and keeping matmuls on tensor\n175\tcores are the levers; compute is small (~15 GFLOP at the largest workload).\n176\t\n177\t### 4.1 Baseline candidate (c001) — robust, per-`(b,c,h)`\n178\t- **Grid:** `B·C·32` programs; program `pid` → `(b, c, h)`, `g = h//4`.\n179\t- Load `Cmat[128,128]`, `Bmat[128,128]` (group `g`); `G = tl.dot(Cmat, Bmatᵀ)` → fp32 `[128,128]`.\n180\t- Load `a[128]`; `cA = tl.cumsum(a_fp32)`; `segsum = cA[:,None]-cA[None,:]`;\n181\t  `L = tl.where(i>=j, exp(segsum), 0)`.\n182\t- `M = G * L` (fp32).\n183\t- Load `X[128,128]`; `Y = tl.dot(M.to(bf16), X)` fp32 accum; store `Y→Y_diag` as bf16.\n184\t- **Config:** `num_warps=8` to spread the `128×128` fp32 accumulators (16384 elts) to ~64\n185\t  regs/thread and avoid heavy spills; `num_stages=2–3`. Everything is 128-wide so no masking\n186\t  on loads/stores (all divisible by 128).\n187\t- Rationale: simplest faithful mapping; establishes a correct, passing reference before any\n188\t  perf tuning.\n189\t\n190\t### 4.2 Optimization axes (later candidates, one change per ID)\n191\t1. **KV tiling / causal skip (flash-style, no renorm).** `BLOCK_M=64` query rows, loop kv in\n192\t   blocks of 64 with causal pruning (skip `j`-blocks strictly above the diagonal; apply the\n193\t   `i>=j` mask only on the diagonal block). Lowers register pressure ([64,128] accumulator →\n194\t   `num_warps=4`), improves occupancy, and skips ~half the score work. Compare vs full-128.\n195\t2. **Group fusion of `G`.** One program per `(b, c, g)` computes `Cmat@Bmatᵀ` **once**, then\n196\t   loops the 4 heads of the group: `M_h = G∘L_h`, `Y_h = M_h@X_h`. Cuts matmul-1 count 4× and\n197\t   `B`/`C` loads 4×. Grid shrinks to `B·C·8` (fewer waves — watch occupancy on small `B·C`).\n198\t3. **`num_warps` / `num_stages` / block-size autotune** across {4,8} warps, {2,3,4} stages,\n199\t   `BLOCK_M∈{64,128}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128}`. Pick per-workload-robust config (must be one\n200\t   immutable kernel across all 5).\n201\t4. **Load layout / block pointers.** `X`/`Y` tiles: row stride `= 32*128 = 4096`, contiguous\n202\t   in `d` (good coalescing on the 128-wide inner dim). `B`/`C` tiles: row stride\n203\t   `= 8*128 = 1024`, contiguous in `n`. `A_cumsum` contiguous in `s` (stride 1). Use\n204\t   `make_block_ptr` / precomputed strides; keep the contiguous 128-dim innermost for\n205\t   coalesced 128-element vector loads.\n206\t5. **Matmul-2 precision toggle** (bf16 tensor core ↔ fp32/TF32) as a correctness/perf knob.\n207\t6. **Epilogue fusion:** compute `L`, `M` in-register and write only `Y` (avoid materializing\n208\t   any `[128,128]` intermediate to global memory — the reference's `G`, `L`, `M` blowups are\n209\t   exactly the \"memory-intensive\" cost this kernel must avoid).\n210\t\n211\t### 4.3 Things to avoid\n212\t- Materializing `L`/`G`/`M` (`[B,C,128,128,32]`) in HBM — that is the naive PyTorch cost we\n213\t  are beating; keep them on-chip.\n214\t- Softmax-style max subtraction (changes the un-normalized result).\n215\t- Factored `exp(cA[i])·exp(-cA[j])` (overflow/underflow).\n216\t- Any non-Triton compute path.\n217\t\n218\t---\n219\t\n220\t## 5. Validation strategy\n221\t\n222\tBecause direct CUDA/torch/profiler execution and any alternate correctness harness are\n223\tforbidden, validation is **analysis-first, evaluator-confirmed**:\n224\t\n225\t1. **Static/index audit (pre-eval).** Re-derive every stride and index against §1.2 before\n226\t   each candidate: group mapping `g=h//4`; `A_cumsum[b,h,c,s]` layout (note `h` and `c` axes\n227\t   are swapped vs `X`); `L` lower-triangular incl. diagonal; `G = C@Bᵀ` (i indexes C, j\n228\t   indexes B); `Y = M@X` reduces over `j`; output layout identical to `X`.\n229\t2. **Numeric-faithfulness checklist.** fp32 accumulation everywhere; exp-of-difference (not\n230\t   factored); no max-subtraction; bf16 cast only at the final store (and at matmul-2 operands).\n231\t3. **First candidate = correctness anchor.** c001 is the simplest faithful mapping; its job is\n232\t   to *pass all 5 feedback workloads* (watch specifically for NaN/Inf from the exp range and\n233\t   for the `A_cumsum` axis-order pitfall). Only after a clean pass do we chase speed.\n234\t4. **One-variable-per-candidate.** Each subsequent candidate changes exactly one axis from §4.2\n235\t   so a correctness regression or speedup is attributable. Record per-workload pass/fail +\n236\t   time, geomean, parent, source hash, hypothesis, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage\n237\t   in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n238\t5. **Convergence / stop.** Stop when speedup plateaus across ~2–3 consecutive candidates or at\n239\t   the budget; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. `final` only on explicit operator approval.\n240\t\n241\t### Open questions to resolve empirically via feedback\n242\t- Does the largest `exp` range on the `random` inputs stay finite (no Inf failures)?\n243\t- Is bf16 matmul-2 within tolerance on all 5 workloads, or is TF32/fp32 needed?\n244\t- Full-128 tile (`num_warps=8`) vs `BLOCK_M=64` flash tiling — which wins on A800 given the\n245\t  memory-bound profile?\n246\t- Does per-group `G` fusion actually help, or does the reduced grid hurt occupancy on small\n247\t  `B·C` (e.g. (1,3) → only 24 group-programs)?\n248\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"186381ab-5278-4fc8-9646-c8c71e3836fb","uuid":"dedb5338-f558-41fe-9d61-ea5442ed252f","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:47:29.325Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/070 Mamba2 Fused Intra-Chunk Diagonal Computation\n\nTask: optimize the official SOL-ExecBench task\n`L1/070_mamba2_fused_intra_chunk_diagonal_computation` on **NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**.\nSubmission is `solution/solution.py` exposing `run(hidden_states, A_cumsum, B, C) -> Y_diag`.\nPrimary implementation must be **Triton** (PyTorch only for metadata/launch plumbing; no\nTorch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback).\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation actually computes\n\n### 1.1 Shapes and constants (from `task/definition.json`)\n\nConstants (fixed across all workloads):\n\n| name        | value | role                      |\n|-------------|-------|---------------------------|\n| CHUNK_SIZE  | 128   | intra-chunk sequence len  |\n| NUM_HEADS   | 32    | attention heads           |\n| HEAD_DIM    | 128   | per-head feature dim (V/out) |\n| N_GROUPS    | 8     | groups for B/C            |\n| STATE_SIZE  | 128   | SSM state dim (contraction dim of G) |\n\nDerived: `heads_per_group = NUM_HEADS // N_GROUPS = 4`, i.e. head `h` belongs to group\n`g = h // 4`.\n\nVariable axes: `batch_size (B)` and `num_chunks (C)`.\n\nInputs (all `bfloat16`):\n- `hidden_states` (X): `[B, C, 128(s), 32(h), 128(d)]`\n- `A_cumsum`      (a): `[B, 32(h), C, 128(s)]`\n- `B` (Bmat):          `[B, C, 128(s), 8(g), 128(n)]`\n- `C` (Cmat):          `[B, C, 128(s), 8(g), 128(n)]`\n\nOutput (`bfloat16`):\n- `Y_diag` (Y): `[B, C, 128(s), 32(h), 128(d)]`  (same layout as `hidden_states`)\n\n### 1.2 Per-(batch b, chunk c, head h) math\n\nThe reference expands B/C over heads via `repeat_interleave(4, dim=groups)`, so group\n`g = h // 4`. For each independent `(b, c, h)` triple define, over local indices\n`i, j ∈ [0,128)` (query row `i`, key/value row `j`) and state/feature index `n, d`:\n\n- `Cmat = C[b, c, :, g, :]`  → `[128(i), 128(n)]`\n- `Bmat = B[b, c, :, g, :]`  → `[128(j), 128(n)]`\n- `a    = A_cumsum[b, h, c, :]` → `[128]`\n- `X    = hidden_states[b, c, :, h, :]` → `[128(j), 128(d)]`\n\n**Step 1 — decay mask `L` (segment_sum):**\nThe reference *re-cumsums* the input `A_cumsum` inside the chunk. Working it through the\n`tril(diagonal=-1)` masked cumsum + `tril(diagonal=0)` `-inf` fill:\n\n```\nsegsum[i, j] = sum_{k=j+1..i} a[k]           for i >= j     (diagonal i==j → empty sum → 0)\nsegsum[i, j] = -inf                          for i <  j\nL[i, j]      = exp(segsum[i, j])             (i<j → 0, i==j → 1)\n```\n\nEquivalently, with prefix sum `cA[i] = sum_{k=0..i} a[k]`:\n```\nL[i, j] = exp(cA[i] - cA[j]) for i >= j, else 0.\n```\n`L` is **lower-triangular (incl. diagonal)** in `(i, j)`, and is **per-head** (because `a`\ndepends on `h`). NOTE: the input is *named* `A_cumsum` but the reference applies a further\ncumulative sum to it — we must reproduce that extra cumsum, not treat the input as already\nbeing `cA`.\n\n**Step 2 — scores `G` (state contraction):**\n```\nG[i, j] = sum_n Cmat[i, n] * Bmat[j, n]   →   G = Cmat @ Bmat^T   ([128,128])\n```\n`G` depends only on `(b, c, g)` — it is **shared by the 4 heads of a group**.\n\n**Step 3 — masked weights `M`:**\n```\nM[i, j] = G[i, j] * L[i, j]     (lower-triangular, per-head)\n```\n\n**Step 4 — output:**\n```\nY[i, d] = sum_j M[i, j] * X[j, d]   →   Y = M @ X   ([128,128])\n```\n\n### 1.3 One-line characterization\n\nThis is **masked \"attention\" per `(b, c, h)`** with:\n`Q≡Cmat`, `K≡Bmat` (contraction over `state_size`), scores `G = Q·Kᵀ`, an\nexponential **causal decay mask** `L` (no softmax / no normalization), `V≡X`, output\n`Y = (G ∘ L) · X`. Every reduction dimension (state, kv-seq, head_dim) is exactly **128**.\nThere are `B·C·32` independent `[128×128]` output tiles; each costs two\n`128×128×128` matmuls (`G`, then `Y`).\n\n### 1.4 Feedback workload sizes\n\n`(B, C)` ∈ {(2,4), (1,3), (4,4), (4,7), (1,4)} → `B·C·H` programs (per-head layout) ∈\n{256, 96, 512, 896, 128}. Largest is `B·C=28` → 896 head-tiles. With 108 SMs on A800 that\nis ~2–8 waves; parallelism is adequate even before any grouping.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Constraints\n\n- **Triton-only compute**; PyTorch allowed for shapes/strides/empty-alloc/launch only.\n- **No fallback** of any kind (Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-ext/alternate). A failing Triton kernel\n  is simply invalid.\n- Output must be `bfloat16`, exact shape `[B, C, 128, 32, 128]`, same layout as input `X`.\n- **Cannot run** CUDA/profiler/`nvidia-smi`/external evaluator/alternate correctness harness\n  directly. The *only* correctness+perf signal is `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`.\n  → Validation must lean heavily on **static analysis and faithful math reproduction**, with\n  each candidate confirmed through the official feedback evaluator.\n- Immutable, sequential candidates `c001, c002, …`; one kernel version over all 5 feedback\n  workloads = one evaluation. Budget: 100 evals; token soft/hard 1.0M / 1.2M.\n- Metric: **geometric-mean speedup**, and **every** selected workload must pass correctness.\n- Skills: `KernelWiki` (Blackwell/Hopper) and `ncu-report-skill` (B200/sm_100 profiling) are\n  **out of scope** for an Ampere `sm_80` target and are additionally blocked by the\n  \"no profiler\" rule; I will not invoke them. All other external agents/tools are forbidden.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Numerical risks and how to match the reference\n\nTolerance (all 5 feedback workloads): `max_atol = 1e-5`, `max_rtol = 0.05` (assumed combined,\n`|out-ref| <= atol + rtol·|ref|`). `rtol = 5%` is loose; `atol = 1e-5` only bites near zero.\n\nReference numeric path: everything is promoted to **fp32** for the arithmetic (A, B, C, X all\ncast to fp32), `L = exp(...)` in fp32, and the final `Y` is cast to bf16 on return.\n\n1. **Exponential dynamic range (main risk).** Inputs are `random` (≈ N(0,1) bf16). `a` is not\n   guaranteed negative, so `cA` behaves like a random walk (std ~`sqrt(128)≈11`), and\n   `cA[i]-cA[j]` for `i>=j` can reach ±(30–60). `exp(60)≈1e26` is finite in fp32 (max ≈3.4e38)\n   but **huge**; extreme tails could overflow to `inf`. Mitigations / rules:\n   - Compute `exp` of the **difference** `segsum[i,j] = cA[i]-cA[j]` directly (as the reference\n     does), **never** as `exp(cA[i])·exp(-cA[j])` — the factored form overflows/underflows even\n     when the difference is bounded (`cA[i]` alone can be ±40).\n   - **Do NOT** use the FlashAttention max-subtraction / online-rescale trick. There is **no\n     softmax normalization here**, so subtracting a row max changes the result (it would not\n     cancel). We must accumulate the true weighted sum. (We may still *tile* the kv loop, just\n     without renormalization.)\n   - Because both our kernel and the reference form the same `exp(difference)` in fp32, `inf`\n     outputs (if any) should coincide; but `inf`/`nan` are a correctness landmine — watch the\n     first feedback run for NaN/Inf failures and, if needed, keep the exp argument computation\n     bit-faithful to the reference ordering.\n\n2. **Matmul-1 (`G = Cmat @ Bmatᵀ`).** Reference: bf16→fp32 (lossless) then fp32 MAC. Triton\n   `tl.dot(bf16, bf16, out=fp32)` uses bf16 tensor cores with fp32 accumulate; bf16×bf16 exact\n   products + fp32 accumulate ≈ the reference to well within `rtol`. Acceptable.\n\n3. **Matmul-2 (`Y = M @ X`).** `M = G∘L` is fp32 with large dynamic range; `X` is bf16 (the\n   reference casts it to fp32 losslessly). Two options:\n   - **bf16 tensor-core** (`M→bf16`, `X→bf16`, fp32 accum): fast; per-element relative error of\n     `M` ≈ 2⁻⁸ ≈ 0.4% — comfortably inside `rtol=0.05`. Since each output element is dominated\n     by the largest-`L` terms (same as the reference fp32 sum), small terms vanish in both.\n   - **fp32/TF32** (`M` fp32, `X`→fp32, `tl.dot` TF32 ~10-bit): closer to the reference but\n     slower. Keep as a correctness fallback if bf16 proves marginal on any workload.\n   Default plan: bf16 tensor core; escalate to TF32 only if a workload fails.\n\n4. **Near-zero atol.** Rows with few unmasked terms (e.g. row `i=0` has only `j=0`,\n   `Y[0,d]=G[0,0]·X[0,d]`) are single products computed almost identically in both paths →\n   safe. The bf16 output quantization the reference itself applies (~value·2⁻⁸) dominates and\n   is inside tolerance.\n\n5. **Group sharing correctness.** If a candidate computes `G` once per group and reuses it for\n   4 heads, the reuse is exact (`G` truly is head-independent); only `L` and `X` differ per\n   head. Must index `A_cumsum[b,h,c,:]` and `X[...,h,:]` per head, but `Bmat/Cmat` per group.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Triton design space\n\nBecause `chunk = state = head_dim = 128`, every tile is a clean multiple of 128; no ragged\nremainders. The op is **memory-heavy**: `X` and `Y` are the big tensors\n(`B·C·32·128·128·2 B` ≈ 59 MB each at `B·C=28`), while `B`/`C` are 4× smaller (8 groups) and\n`A_cumsum` is tiny. So minimizing redundant `X`/`Y` traffic and keeping matmuls on tensor\ncores are the levers; compute is small (~15 GFLOP at the largest workload).\n\n### 4.1 Baseline candidate (c001) — robust, per-`(b,c,h)`\n- **Grid:** `B·C·32` programs; program `pid` → `(b, c, h)`, `g = h//4`.\n- Load `Cmat[128,128]`, `Bmat[128,128]` (group `g`); `G = tl.dot(Cmat, Bmatᵀ)` → fp32 `[128,128]`.\n- Load `a[128]`; `cA = tl.cumsum(a_fp32)`; `segsum = cA[:,None]-cA[None,:]`;\n  `L = tl.where(i>=j, exp(segsum), 0)`.\n- `M = G * L` (fp32).\n- Load `X[128,128]`; `Y = tl.dot(M.to(bf16), X)` fp32 accum; store `Y→Y_diag` as bf16.\n- **Config:** `num_warps=8` to spread the `128×128` fp32 accumulators (16384 elts) to ~64\n  regs/thread and avoid heavy spills; `num_stages=2–3`. Everything is 128-wide so no masking\n  on loads/stores (all divisible by 128).\n- Rationale: simplest faithful mapping; establishes a correct, passing reference before any\n  perf tuning.\n\n### 4.2 Optimization axes (later candidates, one change per ID)\n1. **KV tiling / causal skip (flash-style, no renorm).** `BLOCK_M=64` query rows, loop kv in\n   blocks of 64 with causal pruning (skip `j`-blocks strictly above the diagonal; apply the\n   `i>=j` mask only on the diagonal block). Lowers register pressure ([64,128] accumulator →\n   `num_warps=4`), improves occupancy, and skips ~half the score work. Compare vs full-128.\n2. **Group fusion of `G`.** One program per `(b, c, g)` computes `Cmat@Bmatᵀ` **once**, then\n   loops the 4 heads of the group: `M_h = G∘L_h`, `Y_h = M_h@X_h`. Cuts matmul-1 count 4× and\n   `B`/`C` loads 4×. Grid shrinks to `B·C·8` (fewer waves — watch occupancy on small `B·C`).\n3. **`num_warps` / `num_stages` / block-size autotune** across {4,8} warps, {2,3,4} stages,\n   `BLOCK_M∈{64,128}`, `BLOCK_N∈{64,128}`. Pick per-workload-robust config (must be one\n   immutable kernel across all 5).\n4. **Load layout / block pointers.** `X`/`Y` tiles: row stride `= 32*128 = 4096`, contiguous\n   in `d` (good coalescing on the 128-wide inner dim). `B`/`C` tiles: row stride\n   `= 8*128 = 1024`, contiguous in `n`. `A_cumsum` contiguous in `s` (stride 1). Use\n   `make_block_ptr` / precomputed strides; keep the contiguous 128-dim innermost for\n   coalesced 128-element vector loads.\n5. **Matmul-2 precision toggle** (bf16 tensor core ↔ fp32/TF32) as a correctness/perf knob.\n6. **Epilogue fusion:** compute `L`, `M` in-register and write only `Y` (avoid materializing\n   any `[128,128]` intermediate to global memory — the reference's `G`, `L`, `M` blowups are\n   exactly the \"memory-intensive\" cost this kernel must avoid).\n\n### 4.3 Things to avoid\n- Materializing `L`/`G`/`M` (`[B,C,128,128,32]`) in HBM — that is the naive PyTorch cost we\n  are beating; keep them on-chip.\n- Softmax-style max subtraction (changes the un-normalized result).\n- Factored `exp(cA[i])·exp(-cA[j])` (overflow/underflow).\n- Any non-Triton compute path.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Validation strategy\n\nBecause direct CUDA/torch/profiler execution and any alternate correctness harness are\nforbidden, validation is **analysis-first, evaluator-confirmed**:\n\n1. **Static/index audit (pre-eval).** Re-derive every stride and index against §1.2 before\n   each candidate: group mapping `g=h//4`; `A_cumsum[b,h,c,s]` layout (note `h` and `c` axes\n   are swapped vs `X`); `L` lower-triangular incl. diagonal; `G = C@Bᵀ` (i indexes C, j\n   indexes B); `Y = M@X` reduces over `j`; output layout identical to `X`.\n2. **Numeric-faithfulness checklist.** fp32 accumulation everywhere; exp-of-difference (not\n   factored); no max-subtraction; bf16 cast only at the final store (and at matmul-2 operands).\n3. **First candidate = correctness anchor.** c001 is the simplest faithful mapping; its job is\n   to *pass all 5 feedback workloads* (watch specifically for NaN/Inf from the exp range and\n   for the `A_cumsum` axis-order pitfall). Only after a clean pass do we chase speed.\n4. **One-variable-per-candidate.** Each subsequent candidate changes exactly one axis from §4.2\n   so a correctness regression or speedup is attributable. Record per-workload pass/fail +\n   time, geomean, parent, source hash, hypothesis, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage\n   in `candidates.jsonl` (append-only).\n5. **Convergence / stop.** Stop when speedup plateaus across ~2–3 consecutive candidates or at\n   the budget; then write `SEARCH_COMPLETE`. `final` only on explicit operator approval.\n\n### Open questions to resolve empirically via feedback\n- Does the largest `exp` range on the `random` inputs stay finite (no Inf failures)?\n- Is bf16 matmul-2 within tolerance on all 5 workloads, or is TF32/fp32 needed?\n- Full-128 tile (`num_warps=8`) vs `BLOCK_M=64` flash tiling — which wins on A800 given the\n  memory-bound profile?\n- Does per-group `G` fusion actually help, or does the reduced grid hurt occupancy on small\n  `B·C` (e.g. (1,3) → only 24 group-programs)?\n","numLines":248,"startLine":1,"totalLines":248}}}
